  "prediction": "**Summary of the Report: Implementation of CAPTA Requirements for Substance-Affected Infants**\n\nThe report examines the implementation of the Child Abuse Prevention and Treatment Act (CAPTA) requirements for substance-affected infants. CAPTA provides formula grants to states to improve child protective service systems, including policies and procedures for addressing substance-affected infants.\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n1.  **Increased Number of Substance-Affected Infants:** The opioid epidemic has led to a significant increase in the number of substance-affected infants, with 21,732 cases reported in 2017.\n2.  **State Policies and Procedures:** While all 51 states have policies and procedures in place for addressing substance-affected infants, many states reported challenges in implementing CAPTA requirements, including notification of child protective services (CPS) and development of plans of safe care.\n3.  **Notification of CPS:** 42 states require healthcare providers to notify CPS of substance-affected infants, but some states have varying definitions of \"substance-affected\" and \"plans of safe care.\"\n4.  **Plans of Safe Care:** 49 states reported developing plans of safe care for substance-affected infants, but some states have limited resources and capacity to implement these plans.\n5.  **Challenges and Barriers:** States reported challenges in implementing CAPTA requirements, including lack of clarity on definitions, limited resources, and varying state laws and policies.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n1.  **Additional Guidance from HHS:** The report recommends that the Secretary of Health and Human Services (HHS) provide additional guidance to states on implementing CAPTA requirements, including definitions of \"substance-affected\" and \"plans of safe care.\"\n2.  **Technical Assistance:** The report also recommends that HHS provide technical assistance to states to support implementation of CAPTA requirements.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThe report highlights the importance of implementing CAPTA requirements to ensure the safety and well-being of substance-affected infants. While states have made progress in developing policies and procedures, challenges and barriers remain. Additional guidance and technical assistance from HHS are necessary to support states in implementing CAPTA requirements effectively.",
